Firmware tests on the host
============================

Run from the repository root with PlatformIO Core and a host C++ compiler
(Apple Command Line Tools on macOS):

    pio test -e native

For the standard IDE installation, use ~/.platformio/penv/bin/pio instead of
pio if it is not on PATH. PlatformIO downloads its native platform and Unity
dependency on the first run. No CoreS3 is needed and this command never uploads.

The native build compiles production RobotStateMachine.cpp, RobotAudio.cpp,
TranscriptionProtocol.cpp and TranscriptionUpload.cpp. It excludes Arduino main,
RobotController, the RobotTranscription RTOS/socket adapter, FaceRenderer and real
M5Unified. Audio uses test/support driver/heap/clock fixtures only in the native
include path. TranscriptionUpload uses the same nonblocking I/O interface in the
real socket worker and the native socket fixture. Tests exercise actual ring,
HTTP chunk framing, partial-write offsets, concurrent response parsing and
cancellation decisions; they do not duplicate those production algorithms.

The 22 controller cases use explicit uint32_t timestamps and individual press
events, with no real sleeps. They cover readiness rejection, ten repeated
capture/result cycles, busy/held presses, idle-only sleep, repeated wake/fresh
presses, once-only finalization/completion/failure signals, stale/late IDs,
acknowledgment-coincident busy taps, and admission held during cleanup. Capture
progress continues for 70 simulated seconds, then times out at 1000 ms without
a completed microphone frame (999 ms remains healthy). Actual completion time,
rather than delayed main observation, drives this clock, including unsigned
rollover. The 29999/30000 ms result deadline starts only at valid capture end.
Worker failures while listening and coincident with RECORDED/NO_SPEECH win once;
early or late CANCELLED acknowledgment after quiet capture cannot become a
second error. An already available final result still passes through THINKING.

The eight bounded test_audio cases retain record/replay regression coverage:
queued 20 ms frames, the exact ten-second standalone sample cap, initial quiet,
cumulative activity, silence hangover/reset, energy boundaries, sample-count
playback/borrowing, reusable clip allocation, failed allocation/start/refill,
retry, delayed driver callback, and joined capture-tail truncation. Their test
access fixture reaches the private clip boundary without replacing production
RobotTranscription with a competing class definition.

The 12 test_stream cases use the real audio ring, upload helper and response
parser. They verify 70 seconds of speech plus five seconds of exact silence tail
with byte-for-byte little-endian PCM through ring wrap, a single 40,960-byte
allocation, and no firmware/backend-length cap in capture. They cover driver
release before classification, partial PCM keeping a slot immutable, and slot
requeue immediately after its last PCM byte, even before a stalled HTTP suffix or
any response. Slow classification excludes extra captured tail; a final chunk
cannot precede microphone join. Initial quiet and brief noise cancel without a
final chunk; delayed onset and cumulative 120 ms activity preserve the existing
windows. Exhaustion, allocation/start/refill/stopped-driver failures, live
fragmented HTTP 413, EOF, failed writes and premature HTTP 200 use abort paths.
Logical cancellation stops the microphone and holds admission until the fixture
acknowledges socket cleanup. Write progress resets the five-second stall clock;
first-response and partial-response waits exercise 25- and five-second limits,
including rollover. A status line begun before the terminal chunk remains
premature when its CRLF/headers/body arrive afterward. Readable data cannot
bypass the exact 25-second first-response or five-second response-idle expiry;
24999/4999 ms controls still succeed, including unsigned rollover. Repeated
starts reuse the ring.

The seven test_transcription cases exercise the bounded parser: numeric IPv4
URLs, fragmented multilingual UTF-8, empty/max-size results and embedded NUL,
HTTP rejection/redirects, incomplete bodies, malformed/ambiguous framing,
media/encoding rejection, fixed header/line/body limits, canary-protected output,
and invalid/overlong/surrogate/out-of-range UTF-8.

Firmware compile check (also no upload):

    pio run -e m5stack-cores3

Validate both missing configuration and a configured build: an unconfigured
link can discard the worker and hide integration errors. Use a temporary dummy
LocalConfig.h only when no existing local configuration is present; never
replace or read credentials for this check. See the streaming plan for the
recorded build commands, resolved dependencies and memory output.

Native fixtures model driver completion and socket acceptance, not I2S/DMA
continuity, FreeRTOS queue publication or scheduling, real connect/close calls,
exceptional lwIP close delays, Wi-Fi memory use or physical timing. In particular,
manual fixture cleanup acknowledgment proves the shared audio admission rule,
not SDK close behavior. Configured compilation/linking checks the real adapter;
physical runtime validation remains necessary. Serial output, microphone quality,
first backend PCM versus capture end, queue high-water, minimum internal heap,
PSRAM use, repeated Wi-Fi loss/recovery, long stalls, touch, blink and sleep/wake
remain device checks in docs/plan/stream-audio-while-speaking.md. No device upload
is part of these commands, and a build does not prove hardware behavior.
